<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aiteke Bi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1644–1700</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aiteke Bi was a Kazakh judge and orator of the Junior Juz, one of the three biys traditionally credited with drafting the law code <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhety-zhargy">Zhety Zhargy</a> ('Seven Charters') under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tauke-khan">Tauke Khan</a>, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tole-bi">Tole Bi</a> of the Senior Juz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazybek-bi">Kazybek Bi</a> of the Middle Juz. As a biy he settled disputes between clans by customary law. His sayings are preserved in Kazakh oral tradition, and he is honoured as a national figure in Kazakhstan.</p></div>
